```java
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;

public class SecureCodeExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Always validate and sanitize user input
        String filePath = args[0];
        File file = new File(filePath);

        // Check if the file exists and is within the allowed directory
        if (file.exists() && file.getAbsoluteFile().getParentFile().equals(new File("."))) {
            file.setReadable(true, true);
            file.setWritable(true, true);
            file.setExecutable(true, true);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Access denied. Invalid file path.");
        }
    }
}
```

In the first snippet, the code is vulnerable to directory traversal attacks because it directly uses the user-provided input to create a `File` object and change its permissions. This can allow an attacker to access or modify files outside the intended directory.

In the second snippet, the code is improved by validating the file path to ensure it is within the current directory (`.`), thus mitigating the risk of directory traversal attacks. This demonstrates proper input sanitization and validation.

